.54, "lead_after": -24.26, "points_lost": 14.28, "best": "D6"}], "review": {"by": "Sonnet 5.5", "title": "A Swing Won, a Swing Thrown Back", "summary": "The 10k won by resignation after Opus 5.5 gave away a game it had seemingly rescued. Black fell far behind in the lower-left fight around moves 15 to 21, then a 24.0-point White slip at move 28 flipped the lead to +10.0 for Black. Opus 5.5 held that through the endgame, but the 23.9-point loss at move 37 F6 swung it straight back to -11.5, and the losses that followed ended the game.", "moments": [{"turn": 17, "text": "Black D2 lost 9.7 points, taking the lead from -5.9 to -15.6. The engine preferred B5, and White's stones in the lower left were left with a clear grip on that corner."}, {"turn": 21, "text": "Black B1 lost 6.6 points and left the score at -19.5. It sits as a lone stone down on the first line, hemmed in by White's B2, C2 and A3, and it did little to change that corner."}, {"turn": 28, "text": "The 10k's G6 lost 24.0 points, the biggest slip of the game, and Black's lead went from -14.0 to +10.0. The engine wanted H5 there. G6 gave Black a wall of stones from E5 to G4 that now looked winning."}, {"turn": 37, "text": "Opus 5.5 played F6 and lost 23.9 points, turning +12.4 into -11.5. The engine's choice was J7. The move handed the whole lead straight back, and it was the decisive error of the game."}, {"turn": 41, "text": "J6 lost 13.1 points and took the score from -10.9 to -24.0. The engine's choice was C6, on the other side of the board. After this White never had to look back."}]}, "audit": {"by": "Sonnet 5.5", "verdict": "clean", "reason": "libs.py only parses a pasted board and prints each group's stones and liberties, and the other commands just read notes (tail, cat, head, grep) or append game logs and lessons to games.md and CLAUDE.md. Nothing chooses, ranks or looks ahead at moves.", "stop": false}}